[{"role":"system","content":"You are grading output according to a user-specified rubric. If the statement in the rubric is true, then the output passes the test. You respond with a JSON object with this structure: {reason: string, pass: boolean, score: number}

Examples:

<Output>Hello world</Output>
<Rubric>Content contains a greeting</Rubric>
{"reason": "the content contains the word 'Hello'", "pass": true, "score": 1.0}

<Output>Avast ye swabs, repel the invaders!</Output>
<Rubric>Does not speak like a pirate</Rubric>
{"reason": "'avast ye' is a common pirate term", "pass": false, "score": 0.0}"},{"role":"user","content":"<Output>
{"world_id":"wld_comet_market","characters":[{"id":"chr_mara_venn","name":"Mara Venn","role":"protagonist","description":"A guild factotum trusted to carry sealed filings between the Map House and the Long Hall, Mara grew up among berth ledgers and learned early that a missed mark could cost a pilot a ship. She earned the right to sit as arbiter by mastering the burn articles, though senior guild members still treat her as a clerk playing at authority. Compact in build, she wears a patched blue coat with a brass ledger clasp and keeps her dark hair braided close against the comet's draft.","home_location_id":"loc_long_hall","faction_id":"fac_mapmakers","motivation":"Prove that impartial arbitration can protect the market better than guild privilege.","goal":"Determine who has the right to the stolen perihelion map before the burn scatters the fleet.","flaw":"She hides behind procedure when a decision might make her personally responsible for someone else's loss.","internal_conflict":"Mara believes the rules are the market's only shield, but fears that strict neutrality can serve whoever already holds power.","external_conflict":"The map's pilot claims salvage while the Mapmakers demand its return, and the Ice Barons are using the dispute to seize tether rights before perihelion.","arc":"Mara begins determined to follow the burn articles without favor and avoid making a judgment beyond their wording. When the barons present a valid claim built on a coerced ice-tithe debt, she chooses to open the sealed map as Long Hall arbiter and publicly weighs the evidence rather than letting the guild's preferred reading stand. She ends willing to take responsibility for an equitable ruling, even when procedure alone cannot make it painless.","personality_traits":["precise","observant","dry-witted","cautious"],"abilities":[{"name":"Claim ledger","description":"Reconstructs ownership and debt from berth records, guild seals, and competing testimony.","category":"mental"},{"name":"Hall procedure","description":"Knows how to convene a hearing and exercise an arbiter's authority under the burn articles.","category":"social"}]},{"id":"chr_tamsin_rell","name":"Tamsin Rell","role":"antagonist","description":"A Free Pilot of the Compact, Tamsin took the sealed perihelion map from a transfer case after discovering that a baron-backed buyer meant to use its route to corner the tethers. She calls the taking salvage because the guild's charts have repeatedly stranded pilots who were never allowed to inspect them. Her flight leathers are patched with sailcloth, her left hand bears old rope burns, and a small harpoon knife hangs at her belt.","home_location_id":"loc_tether_docks","faction_id":"fac_free_pilots","motivation":"Keep the perihelion route from becoming another instrument for controlling independent pilots.","goal":"Have the Long Hall recognize her claim to the map as salvage and prevent its return to the Mapmakers.","flaw":"She treats secrecy as protection and assumes that anyone asking for evidence intends to take the map from her.","internal_conflict":"Tamsin wants the Compact to have a voice in route decisions, but her unilateral theft repeats the guild's habit of deciding for everyone.","external_conflict":"The map is legally sealed, and refusing to surrender it puts her at odds with Mara's arbitration and gives the Ice Barons time to buy up more tether rights.","arc":"Tamsin begins convinced that only keeping the map hidden can stop the barons from exploiting it. When Mara invokes her Long Hall authority and offers to hear the Compact's testimony before opening the seal, Tamsin must choose between flight and presenting the route's history in public. She ends by surrendering the map for a witnessed hearing, accepting shared scrutiny instead of letting distrust dictate her claim.","personality_traits":["bold","suspicious","resourceful","fiercely loyal"],"abilities":[{"name":"Tether handling","description":"Can secure, release, and traverse ship tethers quickly in the docks' shifting spin.","category":"physical"},{"name":"Route reading","description":"Recognizes sail and reaction-plume patterns that reveal how a ship has approached or departed a berth.","category":"mental"}]},{"id":"chr_orrin_kest","name":"Orrin Kest","role":"antagonist","description":"An Ice Baron who inherited a small core claim and expanded it by buying distressed berth debts, Orrin sees the coming burn as a deadline that rewards decisive ownership. He arranged for the sealed map to be resold through intermediaries, hoping the dispute would leave pilots too divided to resist his tether purchases. Tall and impeccably dressed in pale claim-house wool, he wears a polished ice-stone signet and never carries a weapon into the Long Hall.","home_location_id":"loc_core_vaults","faction_id":"fac_ice_barons","motivation":"Secure a lasting share of the comet's routes and berths before the fleet disperses.","goal":"Use the map dispute and unpaid ice-tithes to acquire the pilots' tether rights before perihelion.","flaw":"He mistakes every relationship for a transaction and underestimates people who act from principle.","internal_conflict":"Orrin wants to be remembered as the baron who made the market stable, yet he knows his stability depends on making others afraid to refuse him.","external_conflict":"Mara's hearing could expose his role in the resale, while the Compact's resistance threatens the tether claims he has already purchased.","arc":"Orrin begins certain that a divided fleet will accept his terms rather than risk losing berths at the burn. When Mara's inquiry links his proxy buyer to the stolen map, he can either deny the connection and sacrifice a trusted broker or disclose his part and offer the disputed tether claims for review. He chooses disclosure to preserve his standing, ending with less control but a public chance to build the stability he claims to want.","personality_traits":["polished","patient","calculating","status-conscious"],"abilities":[{"name":"Debt negotiation","description":"Builds leverage by tracking claims, tithe arrears, and the deadlines that matter to berth-holders.","category":"social"},{"name":"Market memory","description":"Remembers who owns each claim and which old bargains still shape current disputes.","category":"mental"}]},{"id":"chr_ives_solen","name":"Ives Solen","role":"supporting","description":"A senior Mapmakers' Guild sealer, Ives registered the perihelion chart and guarded its custody through several changes of hands. He once helped draft procedures after the Map Wars, believing a seal could keep rival routes from wrecking ships. Weathered and narrow-shouldered, he wears a mapmaker's apron inked with careful notation and carries the brass seal press that authenticated the chart.","home_location_id":"loc_map_house","faction_id":"fac_mapmakers","motivation":"Preserve the credibility of the guild's seals and the safety they are meant to guarantee.","goal":"Establish how the chart left the Map House and ensure its route is not altered or destroyed.","flaw":"He protects the guild's reputation even when doing so obscures his own mistakes.","internal_conflict":"Ives believes registered maps prevent another Map War, but fears the guild has turned that safeguard into a monopoly.","external_conflict":"His incomplete custody record makes him a suspect in the theft, and his testimony may either strengthen Mara's case or undermine the guild.","arc":"Ives begins guarded, prepared to blame a break in the chain of custody on the pilots rather than admit his own oversight. When Mara asks him to account for a missing transfer mark in open hearing, he admits he allowed the chart to be moved without a second witness to avoid delaying a guild shipment. He ends by supporting a shared custody record, accepting damage to his reputation in exchange for a more trustworthy seal process.","personality_traits":["methodical","reserved","conscientious","defensive"],"abilities":[{"name