[{"role":"system","content":"You are grading output according to a user-specified rubric. If the statement in the rubric is true, then the output passes the test. You respond with a JSON object with this structure: {reason: string, pass: boolean, score: number}

Examples:

<Output>Hello world</Output>
<Rubric>Content contains a greeting</Rubric>
{"reason": "the content contains the word 'Hello'", "pass": true, "score": 1.0}

<Output>Avast ye swabs, repel the invaders!</Output>
<Rubric>Does not speak like a pirate</Rubric>
{"reason": "'avast ye' is a common pirate term", "pass": false, "score": 0.0}"},{"role":"user","content":"<Output>
{"world_id":"wld_lantern_parish","characters":[{"id":"chr_mara_vell","name":"Mara Vell","role":"protagonist","description":"The parish's newly appointed lamplighter, Mara is a narrow-shouldered woman in her late twenties with a peat-stained coat, a burn scar across one palm, and her predecessor's brass wick shears. She grew up among the turf-cutters and left the moor after her younger brother vanished during the Dark Winter's anniversary. Returning to keep the wall, she finds that the work's routines are treated as more sacred than the people who perform them.","motivation":"Keep the wall's lamps burning without letting the parish use the rite to conceal what happened to her brother.","goal":"Discover what night the cut ledger page recorded and determine whether the wall truly went dark.","flaw":"She treats silence as complicity and presses people for answers before she understands what they fear.","internal_conflict":"Mara fears that admitting the rite may be necessary means accepting the parish's habit of sacrificing people to preserve it.","external_conflict":"The wardens ration oil while the council demands a complete account of every lit night, and both resist her questions about the missing page.","arc":"Mara begins convinced that she can force the truth into the open by confronting everyone who withholds it. When a short oil supply leaves one stretch of the wall unlit and the council orders her to certify the night anyway, she must choose between a false entry and publicly naming the shortfall and the people responsible. She names them and keeps a lamp burning with the oil she had reserved for herself, ending willing to share the danger of truth rather than demand it from others alone.","personality_traits":["observant","stubborn","guarded","resourceful"],"abilities":[{"name":"Wickcraft","description":"Trims, sets, and coaxes a stubborn wick to burn steadily in wind and damp.","category":"other"},{"name":"Moor sense","description":"Recognizes safe ground and familiar sounds across the moor, though the bog can distort both.","category":"physical"}],"home_location_id":"loc_keepers_croft","faction_id":"fac_lamp_wardens"},{"id":"chr_ives_harrow","name":"Ives Harrow","role":"antagonist","description":"The senior warden is a spare, silver-haired man whose immaculate cuffs smell faintly of lamp oil. He learned the wall's rounds from his mother and has spent decades keeping the wardens' accounts, measuring each wick and each household's tithe. He was present when the page was cut from the ledger last winter and believes that a carefully maintained silence has kept the parish from repeating the Dark Winter.","motivation":"Preserve the lantern rite and prevent the parish from reopening the panic that followed last winter's missing entry.","goal":"Keep Mara from uncovering the night recorded on the missing page and ensure the wardens retain control of the oil stores.","flaw":"He confuses secrecy with protection and assumes only he can judge what the parish is able to bear.","internal_conflict":"Ives knows that hiding the entry has made the wardens' promises harder to believe, but fears that confession will make the lamps—and the parish—fail.","external_conflict":"Mara's inquiry threatens the wardens' authority, while the turf-cutters challenge his accounting of their oil and the council demands his records.","arc":"Ives begins certain that keeping the missing night secret is the only way to protect the parish. When Mara confronts him with the wardens' short count and refuses to enter a false lit night, he can either let the council blame the cutters or disclose his own part in concealing the page. He gives the council his account and accepts the wardens' loss of authority, ending with the truth exposed but the rite no longer resting on his private control.","personality_traits":["disciplined","paternal","evasive","ritual-minded"],"abilities":[{"name":"Oil accounting","description":"Can spot inconsistencies in a store ledger and estimate how long the wall's supply will last.","category":"mental"},{"name":"Night rounds","description":"Knows the wall's lantern niches and their quirks from years of tending them.","category":"physical"}],"home_location_id":"loc_lantern_wall","faction_id":"fac_lamp_wardens"},{"id":"chr_elspeth_muir","name":"Elspeth Muir","role":"supporting","description":"The council's ledger-keeper, Elspeth is a broad-faced widow with ink-dark fingertips and a shawl pinned with a blunt iron clasp. She records the parish's lit nights at the Moot Kirk and treats the ledger's wording as a civic safeguard: if a night is not entered, no household can be made to answer for what happened. She cut the missing page herself after recognizing a name in its margin, and has since hidden behind the authority of the council's rules.","motivation":"Keep the parish from turning one night's record into a public accusation against a vulnerable household.","goal":"Prevent the council from using the ledger to collect a debt or punish someone for the missing night.","flaw":"She uses procedure to avoid making her own choices visible.","internal_conflict":"Elspeth believes an honest record matters, yet fears that the truth in this record will cause more harm than the lie she helped preserve.","external_conflict":"The council demands an explanation for the missing page, while Mara and Ives each seek to control what the ledger says about the night.","arc":"Elspeth begins hiding behind the rule that an unentered night did not happen, telling herself this protects a household from accusation. When the council presses her to certify a replacement entry that would shift blame onto the turf-cutters, she must choose between procedure and responsibility. She admits that she cut the page and gives Mara the surviving ledger, ending ready to keep a truthful record even when it implicates her.","personality_traits":["patient","cautious","compassionate","formidable"],"abilities":[{"name":"Ledger hand","description":"