{"world_id":"wld_lantern_parish","characters":[{"id":"chr_mara_vell","name":"Mara Vell","role":"protagonist","description":"The parish's newly appointed lamplighter, Mara is a narrow-shouldered woman in her late twenties with a peat-stained coat, a burn scar across one palm, and her predecessor's brass wick shears. She grew up among the turf-cutters and left the moor after her younger brother vanished during the Dark Winter's anniversary. Returning to keep the wall, she finds that the work's routines are treated as more sacred than the people who perform them.","motivation":"Keep the wall's lamps burning without letting the parish use the rite to conceal what happened to her brother.","goal":"Discover what night the cut ledger page recorded and determine whether the wall truly went dark.","flaw":"She treats silence as complicity and presses people for answers before she understands what they fear.","internal_conflict":"Mara fears that admitting the rite may be necessary means accepting the parish's habit of sacrificing people to preserve it.","external_conflict":"The wardens ration oil while the council demands a complete account of every lit night, and both resist her questions about the missing page.","arc":"Mara begins convinced that she can force the truth into the open by confronting everyone who withholds it. When a short oil supply leaves one stretch of the wall unlit and the council orders her to certify the night anyway, she must choose between a false entry and publicly naming the shortfall and the people responsible. She names them and keeps a lamp burning with the oil she had reserved for herself, ending willing to share the danger of truth rather than demand it from others alone.","personality_traits":["observant","stubborn","guarded","resourceful"],"abilities":[{"name":"Wickcraft","description":"Trims, sets, and coaxes a stubborn wick to burn steadily in wind and damp.","category":"other"},{"name":"Moor sense","description":"Recognizes safe ground and familiar sounds across the moor, though the bog can distort both.","category":"physical"}],"home_location_id":"loc_keepers_croft","faction_id":"fac_lamp_wardens"},{"id":"chr_ives_harrow","name":"Ives Harrow","role":"antagonist","description":"The senior warden is a spare, silver-haired man whose immaculate cuffs smell faintly of lamp oil. He learned the wall's rounds from his mother and has spent decades keeping the wardens' accounts, measuring each wick and each household's tithe. He was present when the page was cut from the ledger last winter and believes that a carefully maintained silence has kept the parish from repeating the Dark Winter.","motivation":"Preserve the lantern rite and prevent the parish from reopening the panic that followed last winter's missing entry.","goal":"Keep Mara from uncovering the night recorded on the missing page and ensure the wardens retain control of the oil stores.","flaw":"He confuses secrecy with protection and assumes only he can judge what the parish is able to bear.","internal_conflict":"Ives knows that hiding the entry has made the wardens' promises harder to believe, but fears that confession will make the lamps—and the parish—fail.","external_conflict":"Mara's inquiry threatens the wardens' authority, while the turf-cutters challenge his accounting of their oil and the council demands his records.","arc":"Ives begins certain that keeping the missing night secret is the only way to protect the parish. When Mara confronts him with the wardens' short count and refuses to enter a false lit night, he can either let the council blame the cutters or disclose his own part in concealing the page. He gives the council his account and accepts the wardens' loss of authority, ending with the truth exposed but the rite no longer resting on his private control.","personality_traits":["disciplined","paternal","evasive","ritual-minded"],"abilities":[{"name":"Oil accounting","description":"Can spot inconsistencies in a store ledger and estimate how long the wall's supply will last.","category":"mental"},{"name":"Night rounds","description":"Knows the wall's lantern niches and their quirks from years of tending them.","category":"physical"}],"home_location_id":"loc_lantern_wall","faction_id":"fac_lamp_wardens"},{"id":"chr_elspeth_muir","name":"Elspeth Muir","role":"supporting","description":"The council's ledger-keeper, Elspeth is a broad-faced widow with ink-dark fingertips and a shawl pinned with a blunt iron clasp. She records the parish's lit nights at the Moot Kirk and treats the ledger's wording as a civic safeguard: if a night is not entered, no household can be made to answer for what happened. She cut the missing page herself after recognizing a name in its margin, and has since hidden behind the authority of the council's rules.","motivation":"Keep the parish from turning one night's record into a public accusation against a vulnerable household.","goal":"Prevent the council from using the ledger to collect a debt or punish someone for the missing night.","flaw":"She uses procedure to avoid making her own choices visible.","internal_conflict":"Elspeth believes an honest record matters, yet fears that the truth in this record will cause more harm than the lie she helped preserve.","external_conflict":"The council demands an explanation for the missing page, while Mara and Ives each seek to control what the ledger says about the night.","arc":"Elspeth begins hiding behind the rule that an unentered night did not happen, telling herself this protects a household from accusation. When the council presses her to certify a replacement entry that would shift blame onto the turf-cutters, she must choose between procedure and responsibility. She admits that she cut the page and gives Mara the surviving ledger, ending ready to keep a truthful record even when it implicates her.","personality_traits":["patient","cautious","compassionate","formidable"],"abilities":[{"name":"Ledger hand","description":"Keeps precise entries and remembers how the council's records were altered or amended.","category":"mental"},{"name":"Council persuasion","description":"Can hold a room's attention and make a difficult argument in the language of parish custom.","category":"social"}],"home_location_id":"loc_moot_kirk","faction_id":"fac_moot_council"},{"id":"chr_tomas_reeve","name":"Tomas Reeve","role":"supporting","description":"A turf-cutter in his thirties, Tomas is stooped from years of cutting peat and wears a patched hood that still carries the bog's damp smell. His family has owed oil to the wall for generations, and he knows which paths in the Bog Hollow are firm before dusk. His sister's household was among those left empty by the Dark Winter, so he resents the wardens' claims that every measure of oil is counted fairly.","motivation":"Keep his family from being crushed by an oil tithe that the wardens overstate.","goal":"Make the council examine the wardens' oil count before midwinter call-ins begin.","flaw":"He lets anger turn every negotiation into a contest he must win alone.","internal_conflict":"Tomas wants the wall to protect the parish, but suspects that the rite's costs are being imposed most heavily on families like his.","external_conflict":"The wardens say his family owes oil, the council expects the tithe to be paid, and Mara's search for the missing night risks drawing attention to the cutters' disputed accounts.","arc":"Tomas begins determined to refuse any further oil tithe until the wardens are forced to concede they have over-counted. When he finds Mara at the bog line after dusk, he must choose whether to leave her to the dangerous ground or guide her back without carrying a lantern past the line. He helps her return by memory and sound, then brings the cutters' account to the council, ending ready to challenge the tithe openly rather than make his family bear the cost in silence.","personality_traits":["proud","wary","practical","loyal"],"abilities":[{"name":"Peat cutting","description":"Works the bog's peat with practiced care and can judge a cutting's hazards in daylight.","category":"physical"},{"name":"Bog-path memory","description":"Remembers safe routes through the Hollow by its ground, slope, and sounds without relying on a lantern.","category":"mental"}],"home_location_id":"loc_bog_hollow","faction_id":"fac_turf_cutters"}],"relationships":[{"id":"rel_mara_ives","source_character_id":"chr_mara_vell","target_character_id":"chr_ives_harrow","relationship_type":"rival","description":"Mara needs Ives's knowledge of the wall and its accounts, while Ives sees her questions as a threat to the wardens' authority and to the silence he believes preserves the rite."},{"id":"rel_ives_elspeth","source_character_id":"chr_ives_harrow","target_character_id":"chr_elspeth_muir","relationship_type":"co-conspirator","description":"Ives and Elspeth both concealed the missing ledger page, but each believes the other bears the greater responsibility for the lie."},{"id":"rel_mara_tomas","source_character_id":"chr_mara_vell","target_character_id":"chr_tomas_reeve","relationship_type":"ally","description":"Mara's ties to the turf-cutters give Tomas reason to help her, while he challenges her to see how the tithe makes the rite costly for ordinary households."},{"id":"rel_elspeth_tomas","source_character_id":"chr_elspeth_muir","target_character_id":"chr_tomas_reeve","relationship_type":"uneasy_ally","description":"Elspeth can put Tomas's disputed oil account before the council, but her role in cutting the page makes him distrust her promise of a fair hearing."}]}
</Output>
